=>package.json is a crucial file that acts as the manifest for your Node.js project or application. It includes metadata about the project and manages dependencies, scripts, versions, and other configurations. The Node Package Manager (NPM) uses this file to manage the project's packages and dependencies.

    1. package.json
    Creating package.json
    You can create a package.json file manually, but the easiest way is to use the NPM command:

    --------------------------------------
    npm init
    --------------------------------------

    This command will prompt you to fill in various fields and then generate a package.json file based on your inputs. If you want to create a package.json file quickly with default values, you can use:

    ---------------------------------------
    npm init -y
    ---------------------------------------

    Example of package.json
    Here's an example of a typical package.json file:

    ----------------------------------------
    {
        "name": "my-awesome-project",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "description": "A simple Node.js project",
        "main": "index.js",
        "scripts": {
            "start": "node index.js",
            "test": "echo \"Error: no test specified\" && exit 1"
        },
        "keywords": [],
        "author": "Your Name",
        "license": "ISC",
        "dependencies": {
            "express": "^4.17.1"
        },
        "devDependencies": {
            "nodemon": "^2.0.7"
        }
    }


    ----------------------------------------

    2. Key Sections of package.json

        ->name: The name of your project.

        ->version: The current version of your project.

        ->description: A brief description of your project.

        ->main: The entry point file of your project.

        ->scripts: Scripts to automate tasks. For example, "start": "node index.js" allows you to start your project with npm start.

        ->keywords: An array of keywords related to your project.

        ->author: The author's name.

        ->license: The license under which the project is released.

        ->dependencies: Packages required for your project to run.

        ->devDependencies: Packages required for development, but not for running the project in production.
        -----------------------

        3. NPM Configuration and Commands
        NPM (Node Package Manager) is used to manage packages for Node.js.Here are some common NPM commands:

        Installing Dependencies

=>To install all dependencies listed in package.json:

        ----------------------------------------
        npm install moduleName

        or
        npm i moduleName
        ---------------------------------------
        Example:

        --------------------------------------
        1. npm install express --save

            or
        
        
        2. npm install nodemon --save-dev
        --------------------------------------
    =>Uninstalling a Package
        To uninstall a package and remove it from package.json:

        --------------------------------------
        npm uninstall express --save
        --------------------------------------

    =>Running Scripts
    To run a script defined in the scripts section of package.json:

        ----------------------------------------
        npm run start

            or

        npm start
        ----------------------------------------

    4. Using package.json Scripts
    Scripts in package.json can be used to automate tasks. Here’s an example of a custom script:

    json
    -----------------------------------
    "scripts": {
    "start": "node index.js",
    "test": "echo \"Error: no test specified\" && exit 1",
    "dev": "nodemon index.js",
    "build": "webpack --config webpack.config.js"
    }
    ------------------------------------
    
    ->start: Runs the application using Node.js.

    ->test: A placeholder script for running tests.

    ->dev: Runs the application using nodemon for automatic restarts.

    ->build: Runs the build process using webpack.